/* Usage text of the frame report (unitcmp --frame). */
    "Stack-frame layouts of one function, ours against the reference's, read from the machine code of both\n"
    "(`unitcmp --frame`), so the two frames can be laid side by side:\n"
    "\n"
    "- the prologue: the saved registers, the frame size (`sub esp, n`, or `mov eax, n; call __chkstk`), the frame pointer\n"
    "  (`mov ebp, esp`), the alignment (`and esp, -n`), the /GS cookie's slot and the EH registration (`push -1; push\n"
    "  <handler>; mov eax, fs:[0]`);\n"
    "- every stack slot an instruction reads, writes or takes the address of (`lea`), by its offset from the entry ESP\n"
    "  (the return address at +0x0, the arguments from +0x4, the saved registers and locals below 0), with the access\n"
    "  sizes, the read / write / address counts and, for a local, the distance to the next slot above it (its extent at\n"
    "  most); a slot read through an index register is marked `[i]`. After `and esp, -n` the ESP-relative slots are\n"
    "  counted from the aligned ESP (`A-0x..`).\n"
    "\n"
    "ESP is followed instruction by instruction: pushes, pops, `sub/add/lea esp`, `mov esp, ebp`, `leave`, and each\n"
    "call: a direct call pops what the callee's `ret n` says (the reference's callee: our relocation's target, the\n"
    "reference's rel32), an import call pops nothing for a cdecl library (`compiler.cdecl_import_dlls`, default\n"
    "`msvcr*`) and the pushes since the previous call for any other library (stdcall), an indirect call (a virtual\n"
    "method) pops the pushes since the previous call, any call followed by `add esp, n` pops nothing. A jump records\n"
    "ESP at its target; the code after a `jmp` or `ret` resumes from what a jump recorded there (two passes, so\n"
    "backward jumps count). An offset is as good as that walk: a convention guessed wrong shifts the slots after the\n"
    "call by the same amount on both sides only when both made the same call.\n"
    "\n"
    "The slot map (a second table after the report) pairs the slots by role, not by offset: the instructions of ours\n"
    "and the reference's are aligned (cfg_diff's block mapping, then the instructions of each block pair by mnemonic\n"
    "and operand classes), and each of our slots takes the reference's slot that the aligned instructions touch. One\n"
    "row per pair: both offsets (from the entry ESP, the same base on both sides), the sizes, the access counts and the\n"
    "offset of the first instruction using the slot; a `!` marks a pair whose offsets differ or a slot with no\n"
    "counterpart, and `swap:` lines name two slots that exchanged places. The pairing is as good as the alignment:\n"
    "where the two order a run of stores differently (another schedule), the instructions pair by position in the\n"
    "block and the map shows the slots crossed; read those rows with `unitcmp --diff`.\n"
    ""
